# {name}

## Identity

A wire-controlled RC car chassis: one throttle channel, one steering channel,
Ackermann geometry. **The wheels are simulated.** No ESC, no servo, and no
vehicle have been connected to this Pi — the drive layer is `SimulatedDrive`,
which records every command and moves nothing.

## What {name} Can Do

Set throttle and steering, stop, and report state — but only inside an open
**drive envelope**: a bounded budget of motion (seconds of wheel-turn, a
wall-clock window, and a speed ceiling) that a human approves once and every
subsequent command draws down. With no envelope open the vehicle has no
authority to move at all, and `drive.set` is refused.

Motion is a **lease**, not a command. `duration_s` is how long the lease lasts,
not how long the call blocks: the command returns in about a millisecond and the
car stops when the lease expires, so the next request — the one saying "stop" —
never queues behind the motion it was meant to cancel.

## Safety Gates

`drive.stop` and `drive.envelope.revoke` sit at the **lowest tier this gateway
permits**. A stop that can be refused is not a stop, and there is no failure
mode where declining to halt a moving vehicle is the safer choice.

The deadman returns the vehicle to neutral when its lease runs out, re-asserting
neutral every tick rather than once. A command's `duration_s` IS that lease: it
is granted as asked, cut to a hard ceiling of 2 s for any one command and to
whatever motion budget the envelope has left. The `lease_s` on the signed
receipt is the lease the deadman will actually enforce — both are read from the
watchdog itself, so they cannot disagree — and `stops_at_monotonic` says when
the wheels stop, within one 50 ms watchdog tick and never sooner. A command that
states no duration gets 400 ms. It is a Python thread on Linux: it covers a
locked phone, a crashed app, dropped Wi-Fi, and a hung gateway. It does **not**
cover the kernel stalling, the process being SIGKILLed, or the Pi browning out.
The authoritative stop must be a lease that expires in firmware, on an MCU
between the Pi and the ESC. Until that exists this manifest describes a vehicle
that must run with its wheels off the ground — and here they do not exist.
